cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or these results, please cite the paper named in
  the title below through its DOI, together with the works listed under
  references that your use relies on.
title: >-
  Does Multi-Agent Confidence Predict Task Difficulty, or Only React to It?
  A Pre-registered Test on Perturb-seq
# GENERATED from paper/paper.tex's abstract by scripts/paper/render_publish_description.py -- do not edit by hand.
abstract: >-
  A multi-agent group-generation system, in which language-model agents
  propose, critique and vote over several rounds, spends test-time compute
  (computation at inference) uniformly across tasks as operated here. That a
  single agent's own vote distribution can gate its test-time compute is
  already reported by the CATTS method of Lee and colleagues (2026); we ask
  the multi-agent question: whether the distribution of stated confidences and
  critiques across a group of role-specialised agents predicts how hard a task
  is, which would let the group's compute be allocated adaptively. The testbed
  is a five-agent orchestrator that predicts gene-expression responses in
  Perturb-seq data (pooled CRISPR screens read out by single-cell RNA
  sequencing), inspired by the CellForge system and operated with the MassGen
  framework. We define metrics on the record of a run, among them Agent
  Confidence Entropy (an evenness index of the agents' stated confidences) and
  the change in the agents' mean stated confidence from the first round to the
  last, and a composite, the Task Difficulty Index (TDI). The testbed records
  no agent-by-agent critiques and no per-round winner, so only the confidence
  metrics and their composite are tested. We pre-register five hypotheses,
  each with a criterion: that a sweep of trained models can attain low error,
  taking the best configuration for each task (a best-case bound, selected on
  the data it is scored on), on each of two screens of the K562 cell line,
  Adamson 2016 (CRISPR interference) and Norman 2019 (CRISPR activation); that
  the Architect, the agent that selects the prediction model (the backbone),
  states varied choices, as measured by their Shannon entropy; that one of
  those metrics or their composite ranks tasks, within a screen, by held-out
  error (the mean squared deviation on the twenty most differentially
  expressed genes of the held-out perturbation); and that a composite fitted
  on one screen (its weights estimated by regression on that screen's tasks)
  ranks the tasks of the other. The design is 41 held-out perturbations,
  called tasks (21 from Adamson, and 15 single-gene and 5 two-gene
  perturbations from Norman), a sweep of 27 model fits per task, and three
  runs of the agents per task, each of exactly three rounds. The agents are
  large language models from a pinned roster of Anthropic Claude models (Haiku
  4.5 for the four roles that propose, Sonnet 5.5 for the role that validates)
  whose served model is recorded and asserted per call. A run in which any
  agent step falls back to a rule-based default is invalid, and every run
  records its code revision, configuration, dataset digests and serving
  models. We find that four of the five pre-registered hypotheses are
  supported and the cross-dataset transfer hypothesis is not; every hypothesis
  could be evaluated. The sweep reaches the pre-registered error on both
  screens, and the entropy of the Architect's stated choices is above the
  pre-registered minimum. Within each screen, the drop in the agents' mean
  confidence across rounds, and the composite that includes it, rank tasks by
  held-out error, which meets the pre-registered ranking criterion. From the
  second round on, however, every agent's prompt carries the previous round's
  held-out error, so that drop is stated after an error has been shown. In an
  exploratory analysis, the mean confidence stated before any feedback does
  not rank tasks by error (Spearman's rank correlation ρ = 0.003 on Adamson
  and 0.214 on Norman, both confidence intervals including zero). Read with
  the design and that analysis, the pre-registered pass is consistent with
  stated confidence falling further where a larger error was shown; it is not
  evidence that the group's confidence anticipates difficulty. The composite
  fitted on Adamson did not meet the pre-registered transfer criterion on
  Norman (ρ = 0.286 against a criterion of > 0.4). The point estimates for
  Agent Confidence Entropy run against the pre-registered direction in both
  screens; this observation is exploratory and supports no claim. A probe that
  would observe the agents' confidences and critiques in a single shallow
  round, and a rule that would recommend an agent configuration from it, are
  specified but not evaluated. The values reported in the earlier release
  (v0.5.0) are superseded in full; the appendix lists the defects behind each.
